    {
      "id": "eea3a791-01d6-4ce1-a335-17e3622fae7f",
      "phaseId": "4d4aa26b-6245-4c0f-8bdf-64573a7afbf5",
      "number": 311,
      "shortId": "DA3MC",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "add-separator-to-the-widget-vo",
      "title": "Add Separator to the widget vocabulary",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "Closes gap 5 of the phase description.\n\nCURRENT STATE\n- The widget EXISTS: heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/separator.rs — `Orientation` enum (line 17),\n  `horizontal()` (line 42), `vertical()` (line 47), `length(f32)` (line 53, documented as \"force an\n  explicit span when the parent centers instead of stretching\"). Catalogued in docs/widgets.md:1753.\n- The vocabulary has NO entry for it. `WidgetKind` (heca/src/chrome/view.rs:30-69) lists 30\n  variants; `Separator` is not among them, and it is absent from `WidgetKind::ALL` (view.rs:78-110).\n- docs/chrome-and-ui.md example 2 uses `Separator::new()` between a table header and its body —\n  a description cannot produce it today. A plugin has to fake a rule with a thin sized Surface,\n  which will not follow the theme.\n\nWORK — follow the \"adding a WidgetKind\" path exactly; the guards make most of it mechanical:\n1. Add the variant to `WidgetKind` (view.rs:30).\n2. Add it to `WidgetKind::ALL` (view.rs:78). Note the built-in protection: `ordinal()` (view.rs:113)\n   is an EXHAUSTIVE match, so forgetting it there is a COMPILE error, and the\n   `all_lists_every_widget_kind` test then catches the reverse omission. Do not fight these — they\n   are why a new kind cannot silently render an empty box.\n3. Add a `realize` arm (realize.rs:114+) mapping to the widget.\n4. Properties: orientation (an enum by NAME — horizontal / vertical, default horizontal) and\n   `length`. If T3 has landed, this should come from the generator rather than a hand-written read.\n5. The existing coverage test\n   `every_widget_kind_realizes_to_a_live_widget_except_the_host_only_ones` must pass — it walks ALL\n   and fails on a kind that does not produce a live widget.\n6. Check the fixture that builds a sample node per kind (realize.rs:1660-1680 gives leaves text or a\n   glyph) and give `Separator` whatever it needs there.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT \"just compose it\"\nA separator is themed: colour, thickness and inset come from the Theme. A hand-rolled sized Surface\nin a plugin would hardcode them, which is precisely what AGENTS.md forbids (\"Read ALL styling from\nthe Theme — hardcode nothing\"). The widget exists so nobody has to.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n- `Separator` is in the vocabulary and realizes to the real widget with orientation + length.\n- Both existing guards (`ordinal` exhaustive match, `all_lists_every_widget_kind`) pass unchanged.\n- Coverage test passes with the new kind included.\n- Update view.rs's props-by-kind table + docs/widgets.md, and add it to the showcase.\n- Visible output: build it and have the USER look before closing.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDONE 2026-07-27 — branch `feat/action-arg-declaration`, commits 67fb118 (the kind) + bf8a5c9 (the stretch fix). Verified in the showcase by the user (\"now it works\"). NOT pushed.\n\n## THE WIRING WAS THE EASY HALF\n\n`WidgetKind::Separator` + `ALL` + `ordinal` + a `realize` arm (heca/src/chrome/realize.rs, next to `StatusDot`) that names neither property — `with_props(Separator::horizontal(), node)`. The exhaustive `ordinal` match forced the arm exactly as the task predicted, and `all_lists_every_widget_kind` + the coverage test passed unchanged.\n\n## TWO REAL DEFECTS THE WORK EXPOSED\n\n**1. `length` and `orientation` depended on each other.** To be settable from a description, `orientation` had to become a `#[prop]` builder. But `length` wrote straight onto `width`, assuming a horizontal rule. Properties arrive sorted by name (`props` is a `BTreeMap`), so `length` lands **before** `orientation` — the length would have gone onto the axis the rule runs *across*, leaving the span unset. That is precisely the order dependency `#[prop]` refuses to express (R3: \"if two properties on a widget genuinely depend on each other, fix that widget's setters\").\n\nFixed in the widget: it keeps `length: Option<f32>` as intent and `apply_axes()` recomputes **both** axes from the orientation/length pair. Neither has to come first. The coverage fixture in realize.rs sets both in the order that used to be wrong.\n\n**2. A rule in a centring container did not appear at all.** Caught by the user looking at the showcase. With no length the span axis was `Length::Auto`; a row with `Align::Center` — which most showcase rows use — gives `Auto` nothing to fill, so the rule laid out 1px × 0 and was invisible. The widget's answer had been a doc line telling the caller to pass a `length`: a workaround at every call site, silently producing nothing when forgotten.\n\nFixed in the widget: with no length it sets `align_self = Some(Align::Stretch)`, so it spans whether or not the container stretches its children. An explicit length is a definite size, so it still wins and the container's own alignment places it. The `length` builder's doc changed meaning — it now *cuts the line shorter*, it is not a workaround.\n\n## TESTS (heca-grid-ui/tests/phase_a.rs)\n\n- `a_separators_length_and_orientation_can_be_set_in_either_order` — drives `set_prop` both ways round. Restoring the old `length` body fails it.\n- `a_separator_spans_a_container_that_centres_its_children` — a centring row and a centring column. Dropping the `align_self` fails it.\n- The pre-existing `horizontal_separator_spans_container_width` still passes untouched.\n\n## FILES\n\n- heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/separator.rs — `Orientation` derives `PropName`; new `orientation` prop; `length` stored as intent; `apply_axes`.\n- heca/src/chrome/view.rs — the variant, `ALL`, `ordinal` (30), props-by-kind table row.\n- heca/src/chrome/realize.rs — the arm + the coverage fixture (which sets both props).\n- heca-renderer/examples/showcase.rs — a new row under \"Separator\" with the vertical rule twice: full height (in a centring row) and `length(24)`.\n- docs/widgets.md — the declarative half, the new stretch behaviour, and why this is a widget rather than something to compose.\n- docs/chrome-and-ui.md — the table example called `Separator::new()`, which never existed; now `Separator::horizontal()`, with a note that it reached the vocabulary here.\n\n## GATE\n\n`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features` 0 warnings; `cargo test --workspace` green across 20 suites; showcase builds. No `cargo fmt`. **User confirmed both rules render.**",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
