    {
      "id": "59f5c555-2a36-4247-9216-5002ae4ce55a",
      "phaseId": "4d4aa26b-6245-4c0f-8bdf-64573a7afbf5",
      "number": 313,
      "shortId": "SSCHJ",
      "priority": 7,
      "shortName": "decide-the-fate-of-the-unused",
      "title": "Colour and appearance become overridable props — theme is the default, not a wall",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "⚠️ USER DECISION 2026-07-25 — THIS OVERTURNS A \"SETTLED\" RULE. Do not re-derive the old one.\n\nTHE DECISION, in the user's words: \"color should not be forbidden it should default to theme but be\noverridable as all other props for all widgets.\"\n\n# ✅ SCOPE DECIDED BY THE USER 2026-07-27 — the WHOLE of `Visual`\n\n**Every appearance field becomes overridable: fill, border, glow, radius, font size.** Not colour alone, and not colour+border+glow. The user was given all three and chose the whole of `Visual`, because it is one rule with no per-field exceptions to explain, and the merge mechanism is the same either way — the extra fields cost almost nothing once `Visual` serializes at all.\n\nNote the two unset spellings differ and both must keep meaning \"ask the theme\": `fill`/`border`/`glow` are `Option`, while `radius`/`font_size` use a `0.0 = inherit` sentinel. A serialized `Visual` must round-trip both without turning \"unset\" into \"set to zero\" — that is the one place this scope decision costs real care.\n\n## Token resolution — settled by the assistant 2026-07-27, tell the user if you change it\n\nA theme token NAME is stored **unresolved** and resolved at paint; only raw hex resolves eagerly.\n\n`realize` has no `Theme` (widgets read `cx.theme()` when they paint), so resolving a token name in `realize` would need one threaded in — and worse, it would freeze the colour at build time, so a token-named override would stop following `prefix+Shift+r`. Following a theme reload is the whole reason to prefer a token name over hex, so eager resolution would destroy the thing that makes the preferred spelling worth preferring.\n\n# WHAT IT REPLACES\n- AGENTS.md SETTLED table: \"Is styling a prop? **NO.** Only semantic ViewVariant / ViewSize /\n  ViewAlign + color/glyph **names**; the host resolves the pixels from Theme.\"\n- docs/chrome-and-ui.md §2.6.1 rule C: \"Styling is not a free prop: colors/fonts/alphas\n  come from the Theme/config, never from raw values passed by the plugin.\"\nBoth must be UPDATED IN PLACE as part of this task. Leaving them contradicting the code is exactly\nthe trap that makes the next agent revert the work. (docs/chrome-and-ui.md Part I R2 and §21 already\ncarry the new rule; Part II §2.6.1 rule C still carries the old wording verbatim by design — update\nthe §21 row, not the verbatim text.)\n\n# THE NEW RULE\nAppearance is a normal property, on every widget, like any other. The Theme supplies the DEFAULT;\na description may override it. Unset ⇒ theme, which is already how the widgets behave: `fill`,\n`border` and `glow` are `Option`, and `radius`/`font_size` use a 0.0 = inherit sentinel, so \"not\nspecified\" already means \"ask the theme\" at paint time. Nothing about theme reload changes for\nwidgets that do not override.\n\n# CONSEQUENCE FOR F003/P017/T1 (already shipped, commit 8718718)\nT1 split `Style` into `Layout` (serializable) and `Visual` (deliberately NOT serializable, so\nappearance was unreachable by construction). That premise is now wrong. `Visual` must gain\nserialization and be merged exactly like `Layout` — which is cheap, because T2's\n`merge_layout_props` (heca/src/chrome/realize.rs) is generic and can be pointed at both halves.\nThe `Layout`/`Visual` split KEEPS ITS VALUE as documentation and grouping (caller intent vs theme\ndefault), it just stops being a barrier. Do not undo the split.\n\n# CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TASK\n`PropValue::Color` (view.rs:201-202) stops being dead — it becomes the carrier. Its existing\nrustdoc already promises exactly the right thing: \"`#rrggbb` / `#rrggbbaa`, or a theme color token\nname\". Both spellings work, with the trade-off documented honestly:\n- Raw hex — a literal override, resolved eagerly. It does NOT follow a runtime theme reload\n  (prefix+Shift+r), by definition.\n- Theme token NAME — stored unresolved, resolved at paint, so it DOES follow a reload. Preferred,\n  and what the docs should steer authors toward.\n\n# WORK\n- Add Serialize/Deserialize to `Visual`, to `Color` (accepting \"#rrggbb\"/\"#rrggbbaa\" and a token\n  name), and to `Border` and `Glow` — all of `Visual` is in scope per the decision above. Make sure\n  the `Option` fields and the `0.0 = inherit` sentinels both round-trip as \"unset\".\n- Merge `Visual` in `realize` alongside `Layout`, reusing T2's mechanism.\n- Update AGENTS.md's SETTLED table and docs/chrome-and-ui.md §21's row to state the new rule and\n  that it replaces the old one, with the date.\n- Update view.rs's props-by-kind rustdoc + docs/widgets.md.\n- Tests: an override lands; an unset field still follows the theme; a bad colour string is ignored\n  (realize stays total); a theme reload restyles a non-overriding widget AND one overriding with a\n  token name, but not one overriding with hex.\n- Visible change: the USER verifies in the showcase.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDONE 2026-07-27 — commits eb9e021 (the model) + aec4d8a (the per-widget colours + the first caller), branch `feat/action-arg-declaration`. **Verified in the running app by the user** on the destructive confirm dialog. NOT pushed.\n\n## THE RULE, NOW BUILT\n\nAppearance is an ordinary property on every widget; the Theme supplies the default. The user's rule (2026-07-25) and scope (2026-07-27: the WHOLE of `Visual`, not colour alone).\n\n## PART 1 — THE STYLE HALF (eb9e021)\n\n- `Visual` gains `Serialize`/`Deserialize` + `#[serde(default)]`. `Border` and `Glow` too; `Color` already had serde (hex in, `#rrggbbaa` out) so it needed nothing.\n- `merge_layout_props` → **`merge_style_half<T>`**, generic over the half. Pointing it at `Visual` as well as `Layout` was the entire change on the realize side — which is exactly why keeping the `Layout`/`Visual` split was right. It stopped being a barrier and stayed the honest grouping of caller intent versus theme default. **The split was not undone.**\n- Unset still means \"ask the theme\": the `Option` fields and the `0.0 = inherit` sentinels round-trip as unset, so a widget that overrides nothing is unaffected.\n\n## ⚠️ TOKEN RESOLUTION — I SETTLED THIS WRONG FIRST, CORRECTED BEFORE CODING\n\nI told the user a token name should be stored **unresolved** and resolved at paint, so it would keep following `prefix+Shift+r`. **That was wrong**, and reading the app is what showed it:\n\n> `main.rs` reload_config: \"Retained pane headers bake theme colors / fonts into their tree at build time, and `pane_header_key` intentionally has no theme identity (themes only change on reload). Drop every header so `sync_pane_headers` rebuilds them against the new theme next frame.\"\n\n`reload_config` already drops `chrome_tree` and every pane header. So **resolving eagerly follows a theme reload for free.** Storing unresolved would have meant turning every `Option<Color>` in `Visual` into a source type and touching all 14 paint sites, to buy behaviour the rebuild already gives — and inventing a second mechanism beside the one the app lives by.\n\nSo: `realize` takes a `&Theme`, and a token resolves there. The correction was told to the user, not quietly applied.\n\n**The token vocabulary is derived, not written.** `heca_theme::Theme` is `Serialize` and its colours serialize as hex, so the accepted names ARE the theme's colour fields (`resolve_color` in realize.rs). Add a colour token to the theme and a description can name it the same day, with no table to update. A non-colour theme field cannot be named by accident (it is not a `#`-prefixed string).\n\nA hex literal is exactly the colour it says and does not track the theme — the trade a caller makes, documented plainly.\n\n## PART 2 — THE PER-WIDGET COLOURS (aec4d8a)\n\n`Visual` covers container appearance only. Eight builders carried the note `host_only(\"colour — reachable once F003/P017/T7 makes appearance overridable\")` — `Label::color`, `Icon::color` (×2), `IconButton`, `KeyHint`, `Tag`, `Row::highlight`, `Row::attention_color`. Leaving them host-only would have made the new rule half true, so they are `#[prop]` now.\n\nThe macro (`heca-grid-ui-macros`) learned `Color`: it arrives as text and parses through `Color`'s own `FromStr`. A token never reaches the library — the host resolves it to hex in `prop_to_input`, where it has the theme — so `heca-grid-ui` still knows nothing about themes.\n\n## PART 3 — THE FIRST CALLER (aec4d8a)\n\n`open_confirm` (heca/src/handlers.rs): a destructive prompt renders its message (\"This action cannot be undone.\") in the theme's **danger** colour. Written as the token NAME, not a hex value, so it follows the active theme and a reload — the first override in heca demonstrates the spelling the docs steer authors toward.\n\nThis exists because the capability was otherwise **unverifiable**: nothing in the app overrode anything, so \"look at the modal\" meant \"confirm it is identical\", which told the user nothing and wasted their time. Reachable with `ctrl+b` then `x`.\n\n## TESTS (6)\n\n- the whole of `Visual` lands from a description;\n- an unset field is left alone (not zeroed — the sentinel trap);\n- the same token gives **different pixels under two themes** while a hex gives the same;\n- the vocabulary is the theme's own fields, and `name` cannot be named as a colour;\n- a bad colour is dropped and its neighbours still apply;\n- a token reaches a **widget's own** colour builder and changes what is painted, and follows the theme.\n\n## DOCS — the dead rule corrected wherever it was still stated\n\n`Style`'s and `Visual`'s own rustdoc (both asserted `Visual` was unserializable *by design*), view.rs's props section (now \"Style props\", both halves, with the token/hex trade), docs/widget-architecture.md (§1's \"never raw colors/pixels\", the `realize` signature in the diagram, and the SETTLED row), AGENTS.md's SETTLED row, docs/widgets.md's props section (rewritten, incl. the per-widget builders and the confirm as the first caller).\n\n## GATE\n\n`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features` 0 warnings; `cargo test --workspace` green across 20 suites; showcase builds. **User confirmed the danger-coloured confirm message in the running app.**",
